5×5 lattice of 4×4 blocks. The 16 outer blocks become 2 and the inner 3×3 blocks become 3.\n\n**Construction stages.** The program emits two canvases: first it paints the border ring with 2, then it fills the interior blocks with 3.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    out = g.copy()\n    cells = components(g == 0)\n    boxes = [bbox(c) for c in cells]\n    ys = sorted(set(b[0] for b in boxes))\n    xs = sorted(set(b[1] for b in boxes))\n    border, inner = [], []\n    for c, b in zip(cells, boxes):\n        if b[0] in (ys[0], ys[-1]) or b[1] in (xs[0], xs[-1]):\n            border.append(c)\n        else:\n            inner.append(c)\n    for c in border:\n        for y, x in c:\n            out[y, x] = 2\n    emit(out, \"Colour every cell on the outer ring of the lattice (first/last block row or column) with 2.\")\n    for c in inner:\n        for y, x in c:\n            out[y, x] = 3\n    emit(out, \"Fill all interior cells of the lattice with 3.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each 0‑cell is one connected component, so the cells form a regular rows × columns arrangement of blocks."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A block lies on the border of the lattice if its top row is the smallest or largest of those row values, or its left column is the smallest or largest of those column values."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"stages.** The program emits two canvases: first it paints the border ring with 2, then it fills the interior blocks with 3."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Fill all interior cells of the lattice with 3."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The fact sheet counts are 2×40 and 3×81, and 40 + 81 = 121 = 11², which matches."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"le.** Find the 0‑components and collect the distinct top‑row values and left‑column values of their bounding boxes."}]},"provenance":